---

<DocsLayout title="Observability & incident response" description="What scmle does once a model is serving: metrics, structured logs, windowed signals, fault classification, signed notifications, continuous evaluation, gated replacement and local recovery measurements. Everything here ran locally." current="observability">
  <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Serve and operate</p>
  <h1>Measure it, tell a fault<br /><em>from a worse model.</em></h1>
  <Fragment set:html={html} />
  <h2 id="since">Signals from the running service, and holdbacks</h2>
  <p>The generated service can feed the signal monitors from its real requests and scored rows: set <code>SCMLE_SIGNALS_FILE</code> and the service writes them there. A hashed holdback assignment and a concurrent holdback check, with a sample-ratio guard, can keep a share of cases on the old behaviour so that a change is compared against units that did not receive it. These were exercised locally only.</p>

  <p class="meta">Local evidence only · 0.1 beta</p>
</DocsLayout>
